1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,835
In February 1916, more than twelve hundred German guns

2
00:00:03,835 --> 00:00:06,960
opened fire on a quiet French fortress town.

3
00:00:07,041 --> 00:00:10,374
For ten months, two armies fed nearly three quarters

4
00:00:10,374 --> 00:00:13,001
of a million men into the same few miles.

5
00:00:13,082 --> 00:00:15,562
Germany's top general had a plan that was

6
00:00:15,562 --> 00:00:18,042
not really about capturing ground at all.

7
00:00:18,123 --> 00:00:19,964
This is the Battle of Verdun, the

8
00:00:19,964 --> 00:00:22,083
battle designed to bleed France white.

9
00:00:22,164 --> 00:00:23,888
And this is how the trap he

10
00:00:23,888 --> 00:00:26,124
set swallowed his own army as well.

11
00:00:26,205 --> 00:00:29,241
By the end of 1915, the Western Front had

12
00:00:29,241 --> 00:00:33,165
frozen into trenches from the Channel to Switzerland.

13
00:00:33,246 --> 00:00:36,134
Millions of men faced each other across mud and

14
00:00:36,134 --> 00:00:39,206
barbed wire, and neither side could break through.

15
00:00:39,287 --> 00:00:41,894
In Berlin, the German Chief of the General

16
00:00:41,894 --> 00:00:45,247
Staff, Erich von Falkenhayn, searched for another way.

17
00:00:45,328 --> 00:00:47,674
He believed the war would be won or

18
00:00:47,674 --> 00:00:50,288
lost in France, not in Russia or Italy.

19
00:00:50,369 --> 00:00:52,998
Britain, he thought, was the real enemy, and

20
00:00:52,998 --> 00:00:55,329
France was the sword in Britain's hand.

21
00:00:55,410 --> 00:00:57,919
Break France, and Britain would have no ally

22
00:00:57,919 --> 00:01:00,370
on the continent strong enough to fight on.

23
00:01:00,451 --> 00:01:02,383
His answer was not a breakthrough, but a

24
00:01:02,383 --> 00:01:04,411
place the French would defend at any cost.

25
00:01:04,492 --> 00:01:06,472
That place was Verdun, an ancient

26
00:01:06,472 --> 00:01:08,452
fortress city on the River Meuse.

27
00:01:08,533 --> 00:01:11,160
Verdun sat in a bulge in the French line,

28
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:14,493
ringed by forts and pushing into German-held ground.

29
00:01:14,574 --> 00:01:17,953
It had guarded France's eastern frontier for centuries,

30
00:01:17,953 --> 00:01:20,534
and losing it would shatter French morale.

31
00:01:20,615 --> 00:01:23,208
In his memoirs, Falkenhayn claimed he had told

32
00:01:23,208 --> 00:01:25,575
the Kaiser he would bleed France to death.

33
00:01:25,656 --> 00:01:28,708
Historians still argue whether that Christmas memorandum

34
00:01:28,708 --> 00:01:30,616
was real, or written after the war.

35
00:01:30,697 --> 00:01:33,024
Whatever the truth, his plan relied on

36
00:01:33,024 --> 00:01:35,657
German artillery doing most of the killing.

37
00:01:35,738 --> 00:01:38,298
The Germans called the attack Operation Gericht,

38
00:01:38,298 --> 00:01:40,698
meaning Judgement, and prepared it in secret.

39
00:01:40,779 --> 00:01:43,592
They massed 1,201 guns, two thirds of them

40
00:01:43,592 --> 00:01:46,739
heavy, against roughly six hundred French guns.

41
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:49,224
Trains carried mountains of shells to depots hidden

42
00:01:49,224 --> 00:01:50,780
in the forests north of the city.

43
00:01:50,861 --> 00:01:54,180
The attack was set for February twelfth, but fog,

44
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:56,821
rain and snow delayed it for nine days.

45
00:01:56,902 --> 00:01:59,106
Then, at a quarter past seven on the

46
00:01:59,106 --> 00:02:01,862
morning of February 21, the guns opened fire.

47
00:02:01,943 --> 00:02:04,956
For about ten hours, roughly a million shells

48
00:02:04,956 --> 00:02:07,903
fell on a front only thirty kilometres wide.

49
00:02:07,984 --> 00:02:10,554
Woods were shredded into stumps, and French

50
00:02:10,554 --> 00:02:12,944
trenches simply vanished into the earth.

51
00:02:13,025 --> 00:02:15,505
In the Bois des Caures, Lieutenant Colonel Émile

52
00:02:15,505 --> 00:02:17,985
Driant held on with two battalions of chasseurs.

53
00:02:18,066 --> 00:02:21,235
Driant was killed on February 22, and only

54
00:02:21,235 --> 00:02:24,026
118 of his men escaped from the wood.

55
00:02:24,107 --> 00:02:26,825
In four days, the Germans pushed forward

56
00:02:26,825 --> 00:02:29,067
about four miles toward the city.

57
00:02:29,148 --> 00:02:31,507
Ahead of them stood Fort Douaumont, the

58
00:02:31,507 --> 00:02:34,108
largest and strongest fort guarding Verdun.

59
00:02:34,189 --> 00:02:36,751
Its layers of concrete and earth had been built

60
00:02:36,751 --> 00:02:39,149
to withstand the heaviest shells of the age.

61
00:02:39,230 --> 00:02:42,352
Yet in 1915, the French had stripped most of

62
00:02:42,352 --> 00:02:45,190
its guns for use elsewhere on the front.

63
00:02:45,271 --> 00:02:48,011
On February 25, about one hundred German

64
00:02:48,011 --> 00:02:51,231
soldiers walked into the fort almost unopposed.

65
00:02:51,312 --> 00:02:53,714
Inside, they found a warrant officer and about

66
00:02:53,714 --> 00:02:56,272
twenty-five men, who surrendered without a fight.

67
00:02:56,353 --> 00:02:59,042
In Germany, church bells rang, and newspapers

68
00:02:59,042 --> 00:03:01,313
hailed the capture as a great victory.

69
00:03:01,394 --> 00:03:04,501
Across France, the loss was a humiliation, and it

70
00:03:04,501 --> 00:03:07,354
would take eight months to win the fort back.

71
00:03:07,435 --> 00:03:10,297
That same night, General Philippe Pétain took

72
00:03:10,297 --> 00:03:12,395
command of the defence of Verdun.

73
00:03:12,476 --> 00:03:14,982
He was a careful infantry general who

74
00:03:14,982 --> 00:03:18,436
believed firepower, not courage alone, won battles.

75
00:03:18,517 --> 00:03:20,534
His first order was simple:

76
00:03:20,534 --> 00:03:22,477
there would be no retreat.

77
00:03:22,558 --> 00:03:25,314
Pétain's real problem was that Verdun had only one

78
00:03:25,314 --> 00:03:27,518
road and one narrow railway to the rear.

79
00:03:27,599 --> 00:03:29,843
That road ran south to the railhead at

80
00:03:29,843 --> 00:03:32,559
Bar-le-Duc, and it became the city's lifeline.

81
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:35,494
The French gathered about 3,500 trucks and

82
00:03:35,494 --> 00:03:37,600
kept them moving day and night.

83
00:03:37,681 --> 00:03:41,423
By March, one truck passed every fourteen seconds,

84
00:03:41,423 --> 00:03:44,641
carrying men, shells and food to the front.

85
00:03:44,722 --> 00:03:47,742
In its first month, the road delivered

86
00:03:47,742 --> 00:03:50,682
about 263,000 men to the battlefield.

87
00:03:50,763 --> 00:03:52,642
The soldiers came to call it

88
00:03:52,642 --> 00:03:54,723
La Voie Sacrée, the Sacred Way.

89
00:03:54,804 --> 00:03:58,200
Pétain also rotated divisions in and out quickly,

90
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:00,764
a system the French called the noria.

91
00:04:00,845 --> 00:04:03,194
Fresh troops marched up toward the guns while

92
00:04:03,194 --> 00:04:05,805
exhausted survivors trudged back down beside them.

93
00:04:05,886 --> 00:04:08,081
By May, forty-two French divisions had already

94
00:04:08,081 --> 00:04:09,846
passed through the furnace of Verdun.

95
00:04:09,927 --> 00:04:12,474
Over the year, about three quarters of

96
00:04:12,474 --> 00:04:14,887
the entire French Army fought there.

97
00:04:14,968 --> 00:04:17,532
The burden spread across the whole nation, and

98
00:04:17,532 --> 00:04:19,928
almost every French family felt its weight.

99
00:04:20,009 --> 00:04:22,989
On April 10, Pétain's order of the day ended

100
00:04:22,989 --> 00:04:25,969
with a famous line: Courage, we'll get them.

101
00:04:26,050 --> 00:04:28,335
Unable to break through on the east bank,

102
00:04:28,335 --> 00:04:31,010
the Germans widened the attack across the river.

103
00:04:31,091 --> 00:04:33,632
In March, they struck the west bank, where

104
00:04:33,632 --> 00:04:36,052
French guns had been raking their flank.

105
00:04:36,132 --> 00:04:39,921
Two hills became killing grounds: Hill 304, and a

106
00:04:39,921 --> 00:04:43,092
ridge called Le Mort Homme, the Dead Man.

107
00:04:43,173 --> 00:04:45,500
For weeks, the same slopes were taken,

108
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:48,133
lost and retaken under endless bombardment.

109
00:04:48,214 --> 00:04:51,326
On May 4, the Germans seized the northern slope

110
00:04:51,326 --> 00:04:54,174
of Hill 304, but the French held the crest.

111
00:04:54,255 --> 00:04:56,874
Soldiers began to describe the battlefield as a

112
00:04:56,874 --> 00:04:59,215
mill that ground up everyone sent into it.

113
00:04:59,296 --> 00:05:02,102
Men lived in flooded shell holes, often cut

114
00:05:02,102 --> 00:05:04,256
off from food and water for days.

115
00:05:04,337 --> 00:05:07,989
Messages often travelled by runner or carrier pigeon,

116
00:05:07,989 --> 00:05:11,297
because telephone wires were cut almost at once.

117
00:05:11,378 --> 00:05:14,106
In June, the fighting reached Fort Vaux, the

118
00:05:14,106 --> 00:05:16,338
next fortress on the road to Verdun.

119
00:05:16,419 --> 00:05:19,476
Its commander, Major Sylvain-Eugène Raynal, had taken

120
00:05:19,476 --> 00:05:21,379
over the fort only weeks earlier.

121
00:05:21,460 --> 00:05:24,054
On June 2, German troops climbed onto

122
00:05:24,054 --> 00:05:27,420
the fort's roof and attacked with flamethrowers.

123
00:05:27,501 --> 00:05:29,750
The battle moved underground, where men

124
00:05:29,750 --> 00:05:32,461
fought from barricades in pitch-dark corridors.

125
00:05:32,542 --> 00:05:34,390
Soon the water ran out, and some of

126
00:05:34,390 --> 00:05:36,502
what remained turned out to be poisoned.

127
00:05:36,583 --> 00:05:39,063
Raynal released his last carrier pigeon, Le

128
00:05:39,063 --> 00:05:41,543
Vaillant, with a desperate plea for relief.

129
00:05:41,624 --> 00:05:44,130
Badly gassed, the pigeon reached the citadel and

130
00:05:44,130 --> 00:05:46,584
died, later honoured with the Legion of Honour.

131
00:05:46,665 --> 00:05:48,252
On June 7, out of water,

132
00:05:48,252 --> 00:05:51,625
food and ammunition, Raynal's garrison surrendered.

133
00:05:51,706 --> 00:05:54,132
Crown Prince Wilhelm was so impressed that he

134
00:05:54,132 --> 00:05:56,666
presented Raynal with a French officer's sword.

135
00:05:56,747 --> 00:05:59,605
Falkenhayn's plan was failing on its own terms,

136
00:05:59,605 --> 00:06:02,707
because German losses were climbing almost as fast.

137
00:06:02,788 --> 00:06:05,297
Every French counterattack had to be met by

138
00:06:05,297 --> 00:06:07,748
German infantry, who died in the same mud.

139
00:06:07,829 --> 00:06:10,156
Still, the German command pressed on toward the

140
00:06:10,156 --> 00:06:11,789
last ridges in front of the city.

141
00:06:11,870 --> 00:06:14,954
On June 22, German guns fired more than

142
00:06:14,954 --> 00:06:18,830
116,000 shells filled with a new gas, diphosgene.

143
00:06:18,911 --> 00:06:21,052
The Germans called it Green Cross, after

144
00:06:21,052 --> 00:06:22,871
the marking painted on each shell.

145
00:06:22,952 --> 00:06:26,203
More than 1,600 French soldiers were struck down

146
00:06:26,203 --> 00:06:28,912
as the gas crept across their positions.

147
00:06:28,993 --> 00:06:32,167
The next day, about 80,000 German infantry surged

148
00:06:32,167 --> 00:06:34,953
forward and captured the village of Fleury.

149
00:06:35,034 --> 00:06:37,044
It was the closest the German army

150
00:06:37,044 --> 00:06:38,994
would ever come to taking Verdun.

151
00:06:39,075 --> 00:06:41,983
That day, General Robert Nivelle issued an order to

152
00:06:41,983 --> 00:06:44,035
his men: You will not let them pass.

153
00:06:44,116 --> 00:06:46,657
The phrase became They shall not pass, and

154
00:06:46,657 --> 00:06:49,077
it was later wrongly credited to Pétain.

155
00:06:49,157 --> 00:06:51,691
Fleury changed hands sixteen times that summer,

156
00:06:51,691 --> 00:06:54,117
until almost nothing of the village remained.

157
00:06:54,198 --> 00:06:56,547
On July 11, the Germans launched one

158
00:06:56,547 --> 00:06:59,158
last major attack against Fort Souville.

159
00:06:59,239 --> 00:07:01,858
About thirty German soldiers reached the top of

160
00:07:01,858 --> 00:07:04,199
the fort, less than two miles from Verdun.

161
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:06,108
They were driven off, and the German

162
00:07:06,108 --> 00:07:08,240
offensive at Verdun had reached its limit.

163
00:07:08,321 --> 00:07:11,653
Far to the east, Russia's Brusilov Offensive,

164
00:07:11,653 --> 00:07:15,281
launched on June 4, smashed into Austria-Hungary.

165
00:07:15,362 --> 00:07:17,740
Then, on July 1, British and French

166
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:20,322
armies attacked along the River Somme.

167
00:07:20,403 --> 00:07:23,144
Germany now had to pull guns and divisions

168
00:07:23,144 --> 00:07:25,363
away from Verdun to plug the gaps.

169
00:07:25,444 --> 00:07:28,048
Falkenhayn ordered his armies at Verdun to

170
00:07:28,048 --> 00:07:30,404
stop attacking and hold what they had.

171
00:07:30,485 --> 00:07:33,142
On August 29, the Kaiser replaced him

172
00:07:33,142 --> 00:07:36,445
with Paul von Hindenburg and Erich Ludendorff.

173
00:07:36,526 --> 00:07:38,534
Now the French struck back, led by

174
00:07:38,534 --> 00:07:41,486
Nivelle and the aggressive General Charles Mangin.

175
00:07:41,567 --> 00:07:43,670
They used a creeping barrage, a wall of

176
00:07:43,670 --> 00:07:46,527
shells moving just ahead of their advancing infantry.

177
00:07:46,608 --> 00:07:48,762
On October 24, after a six-day

178
00:07:48,762 --> 00:07:52,568
bombardment, French troops recaptured Fort Douaumont.

179
00:07:52,649 --> 00:07:54,233
In two days, they took

180
00:07:54,233 --> 00:07:56,609
more than 6,000 German prisoners.

181
00:07:56,690 --> 00:07:59,499
On November 2, the Germans abandoned Fort Vaux,

182
00:07:59,499 --> 00:08:01,650
and it too returned to French hands.

183
00:08:01,731 --> 00:08:04,243
A final French attack began on December

184
00:08:04,243 --> 00:08:06,691
15, behind more than a million shells.

185
00:08:06,772 --> 00:08:10,713
It captured over 11,000 prisoners and 115 guns,

186
00:08:10,713 --> 00:08:13,732
and the battle ended on December 18.

187
00:08:13,813 --> 00:08:16,647
After ten months, the front line stood close

188
00:08:16,647 --> 00:08:18,773
to where it had been in February.

189
00:08:18,854 --> 00:08:22,044
Verdun cost France around 377,000

190
00:08:22,044 --> 00:08:25,814
casualties, and Germany around 337,000.

191
00:08:25,895 --> 00:08:28,875
Together, that is roughly 714,000 men

192
00:08:28,875 --> 00:08:31,855
killed, wounded, missing or captured.

193
00:08:31,936 --> 00:08:34,267
In the first five months alone, the two

194
00:08:34,267 --> 00:08:36,896
armies fired more than forty million shells.

195
00:08:36,977 --> 00:08:39,578
Falkenhayn had meant to bleed France white,

196
00:08:39,578 --> 00:08:41,937
but Germany had bled almost as heavily.

197
00:08:42,018 --> 00:08:44,053
Nine villages were wiped off the map,

198
00:08:44,053 --> 00:08:45,978
and six of them were never rebuilt.

199
00:08:46,059 --> 00:08:49,307
Even today, each of those empty villages keeps an

200
00:08:49,307 --> 00:08:53,019
appointed mayor, caretaker of a place with no residents.

201
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:55,642
Near the old forts stands the

202
00:08:55,642 --> 00:08:59,060
Douaumont Ossuary, inaugurated in 1932.

203
00:08:59,141 --> 00:09:01,433
It holds the bones of at least

204
00:09:01,433 --> 00:09:05,101
130,000 unidentified French and German soldiers.

205
00:09:05,182 --> 00:09:07,998
In front of it lie more than 16,000 graves,

206
00:09:07,998 --> 00:09:11,142
the largest French military cemetery of the war.

207
00:09:11,223 --> 00:09:13,634
The battle created heroes, but some

208
00:09:13,634 --> 00:09:16,183
of its lessons would prove dangerous.

209
00:09:16,264 --> 00:09:20,282
Nivelle's success won him command of the French Army,

210
00:09:20,282 --> 00:09:24,224
but his 1917 offensive ended in disaster and mutiny.

211
00:09:24,305 --> 00:09:26,646
Pétain was called in to restore order, and

212
00:09:26,646 --> 00:09:29,265
France remembered him as the saviour of Verdun.

213
00:09:29,346 --> 00:09:32,558
In 1940, that reputation carried him to power as

214
00:09:32,558 --> 00:09:36,306
head of the Vichy regime that collaborated with Germany.

215
00:09:36,387 --> 00:09:38,488
Verdun was meant to be a machine for

216
00:09:38,488 --> 00:09:41,347
killing Frenchmen while German guns did the work.

217
00:09:41,428 --> 00:09:43,877
Instead, it became a mill that ground up

218
00:09:43,877 --> 00:09:46,388
both armies, for almost no ground at all.

219
00:09:46,469 --> 00:09:49,480
For France, holding Verdun became proof that the

220
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:52,429
nation could suffer almost anything and endure.

221
00:09:52,510 --> 00:09:55,588
Falkenhayn wanted France to bleed white, but at

222
00:09:55,588 --> 00:09:58,470
Verdun, both nations bled into the same mud.
